INT. COMPETITIVE CHILI COOK-OFF - ALLEY BEHIND THE STAGE - DAY

Folding tables. Giant steel pots. A smoky haze. A banner flaps: COUNTY CHILI CHAMPIONSHIP.

LEROY PIKE (40s, apron reading KISS THE COOK) tastes from a ladle and winces. Beside him, his teammate DARLENE "DEE" OKONJO (40s, bandanna) stares into a second pot. Facing them from the next table, rival cooks HAROLD TUCKER (50s, mustache) and WANDERLEY "WANDY" SOUSA (50s, cowboy hat) crack their knuckles.

LEROY
It's missing something.

DEE
It's missing nothing.

LEROY
It's missing salt.

DEE
I added salt.

LEROY
When?

DEE
Before.

LEROY
Before what?

DEE
Before you added salt.

LEROY
I didn't add salt.

DEE
Yes, you did.

LEROY
Then why does it taste like it's missing salt?

DEE
Because you added too much.

LEROY
But you said it's missing --

DEE
Leroy.

LEROY
Yes?

DEE
Stop talking.

HAROLD
Trouble in paradise?

DEE
Mind your pot, Harold.

HAROLD
My pot's perfect.

WANDY
It's a little thin, Harold.

HAROLD
It's not thin. It's rustic.

WANDY
Rustic is a polite word for thin.

LEROY
(to Dee)
He called him thin.

DEE
Who called who thin?

LEROY
Wandy called Harold's chili thin.

DEE
Good. Because he is.

HAROLD
I heard that. He's not thin either!

WANDY
Who's not thin?

HAROLD
Your chili!

WANDY
My chili is magnificent.

LEROY
Your chili is a soup.

WANDY
Your chili is a crime.

DEE
Leroy, don't.

LEROY
He started it.

DEE
He didn't. You did. He said it. Then he said it to him, and he said it back.

LEROY
Who?

DEE
Nobody. Just taste your own chili.

Leroy dips a spoon and tastes. His eyes widen. Dee tastes. She nods slowly.

DEE (CONT'D)
That's not salt.

LEROY
That's cinnamon.

DEE
We put cinnamon in it?

LEROY
We don't use cinnamon.

DEE
Then who did?

They both turn. Across the alley, Harold and Wandy are suddenly very interested in their ladles.

LEROY
You.

HAROLD
Us?

LEROY
You put cinnamon in our chili.

HAROLD
We did no such thing.

WANDY
(to Harold)
Did we?

HAROLD
(to Wandy)
He did.

WANDY
I did not!

HAROLD
Then who?

An airhorn blares from the stage.

Nobody moves. Four ladles hover over four steaming pots.

DEE
Fine. We call it a truce.

LEROY
A truce.

HAROLD
Fine.

WANDY
Fine.

DEE
Fine.

They all lower their ladles at once and begin to stir in perfect, furious silence.
